# No Destructive Database Operations Without Explicit Approval

A single destructive DB command can wipe irreplaceable data in seconds. Unless you can point at a **current** backup, treat any reset as **irreversible** — a local dev database typically has neither backups nor WAL archiving. Convenience is never a reason to run one. The default is always: **stop and ask the user first.**

## Hard stop — never run these without explicit, per-incident user approval

The following are forbidden unless the user, in the current conversation, explicitly tells you to run that specific command after you described its consequences:

- `prisma migrate reset` (with or without `--force`)
- `prisma db push --force-reset` / `--accept-data-loss`
- `DROP DATABASE`, `DROP SCHEMA ... CASCADE`, `TRUNCATE`
- `DELETE FROM` / `UPDATE` without a narrowly scoped `WHERE`
- dropping or recreating a Docker volume that backs a database (`docker volume rm`, `docker compose down -v`)
- any command whose output or docs say it will "reset", "drop", "wipe", "recreate", or "all data will be lost"
- a **scratch-database parameter pointed at a real database** — `--shadow-database-url` (or a schema's `shadowDatabaseUrl`), `--from-url`, `--to-url`, or any equivalent, aimed at anything that is, or could resolve to, the primary `DATABASE_URL` or another populated database.

**The scratch-database trap deserves its own paragraph**, because it is the one destructive operation that announces nothing. A tool treats a shadow database as **disposable**: it wipes it and replays the migration history from scratch to compute a diff. The command never prints "reset", "drop" or "data will be lost", so it slips straight past the pattern-matching above — while doing exactly that to whatever you named. This is not hypothetical: passing the live database URL as the shadow target once wiped every row of a shared dev database here. The schema survived, because it was replayed; the data did not, and what had been created since the last backup was gone.

```bash
# ❌ BAD — the live database doubles as the disposable shadow DB, so it gets wiped
prisma migrate diff … --shadow-database-url "$DATABASE_URL" --script

# ✅ GOOD — a genuinely separate, disposable database as the shadow target
prisma migrate diff … --shadow-database-url "<dedicated scratch database>" --script

# ✅ BETTER for an isolated additive change — skip the diff tool entirely,
# hand-write migration.sql, then apply it forward-only.
```

Before passing any value to such a parameter, compare it against the live URL **and** against every other populated database (a per-branch fork counts). If it matches, or plausibly resolves to the same place, stop and use a dedicated disposable database — this project provisions one for exactly this purpose (`worktree-dev-workflow` → "Shared database & migrations").

"Explicit approval" means a clear yes to *this* destructive action. A general instruction like "implement the plan", "run the migration", or "make the tests pass" is **not** approval to reset or drop anything.

> **Related — availability, not just data:** this rule guards against *data loss*. A plain, non-data-destroying stop or restart of shared services is a separate hazard with the same "stop and ask first" default — see `general-shared-state-and-handoff`.

## When a tool *wants* to reset — stop, do not comply automatically

Prisma (and similar tools) will sometimes demand a reset, e.g.:

> The migration `…` was modified after it was applied.
> We need to reset the "public" schema … All data will be lost.

This is exactly the trap. Do **not** run the reset. Instead:

1. Stop immediately.
2. Report the message verbatim and explain what a reset would destroy.
3. Offer non-destructive alternatives and let the user choose.

```bash
# ❌ BAD — destroys all data because a migration drifted
prisma migrate reset --force

# ✅ GOOD — diagnose first, no data loss
prisma migrate status
# then report findings and ask the user how to proceed
```

## Non-destructive alternatives to reach for first

- `prisma migrate status` to understand drift before doing anything.
- For a *modified-after-applied* migration: prefer reconciling the migration history (e.g. `prisma migrate resolve`) or creating a **new** forward migration — never a reset of the dev DB.
- Develop schema changes against a **scratch/shadow** database, not the populated dev DB.
- If a reset truly seems unavoidable, propose taking a dump first (`pg_dump`) and get approval for both the dump and the reset.

## If a destructive command was already run

Be transparent immediately: state plainly what was run, what was lost, and the realistic recovery options. Do not minimize or hide it.

## Verification checklist before any DB command

- [ ] Does this command delete, drop, truncate, reset, or overwrite data? If yes → it needs explicit per-incident approval.
- [ ] Did the user approve *this specific* destructive action in this conversation (not a general "go ahead")?
- [ ] Did I try `migrate status` / a non-destructive path first?
- [ ] If a tool demanded a reset, did I stop and ask instead of complying?
- [ ] Does this command take a scratch-database parameter (`--shadow-database-url`, `--from-url`, `--to-url`, …)? If yes → verified it names a genuinely disposable database, not the live URL and not any other populated one.
